// §212 — HINDI. Keyed by the English source (normalized innerHTML for rich
// blocks, text for plate labels); see src/explain-i18n.js for why.
//
// The register is a DECISION, and it splits: part names are the transliterated
// loanwords Hindi engineering prose actually uses — एस्केपमेंट, बैलेंस व्हील,
// हेयरस्प्रिंग, मेनस्प्रिंग, बैरल, पैलेट, पिनियन, रैचेट — while physical
// quantities take the native scientific vocabulary that is equally standard:
// आवृत्ति, आयाम, जड़त्व, बलाघूर्ण. Everyday objects keep their everyday word
// (सूइयाँ for the hands, दाँत, हथौड़ा, चेन). A Sanskritic coinage for a part
// would read as invented; a loanword for a quantity would read as lazy. It is
// the SAME register tier one's chrome uses (src/i18n.js) — one movement cannot
// have two names for a part.
//
// NUMBERS AND <code> ARE NOT TRANSLATED — they are the source constants this
// page exists to quote, and tools/explain-i18n.mjs fails the build if one
// drifts. Hindi's digits here are LATIN, which is hi-IN's own default and not
// a concession: Devanagari digits appear only under an explicit -u-nu-deva.
// "मॉडल किया गया" vs "सिमुलेट किया गया" tracks the repo's modelled/simulated
// distinction exactly, and never collapses the two into one word.
